    <section class="procedure-section">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="section-header center"> <span class="section-label">INSPECTION WORKFLOW</span>
                <h2>NFT Inspection Procedure</h2>
                <p>Systematic approach ensuring safe, efficient boiler tube inspection per ASME and EPRI guidelines</p>
            </div>
            <div class="procedure-timeline">
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">1</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Pre-Outage Engineering & Planning</h3>
                        <p>Review boiler/HRSG as-built drawings, tube material specifications (ASME material test reports), and prior inspection/failure history. Identify high-risk tube zones based on operating parameters (temperature, pressure, flow velocity), water chemistry data (pH, oxygen, iron transport), and EPRI degradation mechanism assessments. Develop inspection scope prioritizing critical areas: economizer FAC-prone sections, waterwall lower furnace erosion zones, superheater/reheater creep-susceptible tubes. Coordinate with plant outage schedule—NFT typically performed early in outage allowing time for tube replacement if critical degradation detected.</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> NFT Inspection Plan & High-Risk Zone Map </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">2</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Boiler/HRSG Preparation & Safety</h3>
                        <p>Depressurize boiler and establish lockout/tagout (LOTO) per OSHA 1910.147 and plant safety procedures. Cool unit to safe access temperature (<50°C / 120°F) unless high-temperature NFT specified. Open access doors, handholes, and inspection ports for tube entry points. Establish scaffolding, work platforms, and fall protection per OSHA 1926 Subpart M if required for elevated tube access. Verify atmospheric testing if confined space entry required (economizer housing, convection section enclosures)—oxygen>19.5%, LEL <10%, toxic gases within limits per OSHA 1910.146. Coordinate with plant operations, maintenance, and safety departments for hot work permits and outage status updates.</p>
                                    <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Safety Permits & Access Preparation Documentation </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">3</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>NFT System Calibration</h3>
                        <p>Assemble NFT instrumentation and verify battery charge/AC power supply. Select appropriate probe diameter for tube OD (probe should fit with minimal clearance—typically 2-3mm diametral gap). Perform reference calibration using tube standards with known wall thickness: (1) 100% wall (new tube condition), (2) 80% wall, (3) 60% wall, (4) 40% wall. Measure and record signal amplitude (voltage) for each standard establishing calibration curve correlating amplitude to remaining wall thickness. Verify probe response linearity and repeatability. Set alarm thresholds for automated flagging of tubes exceeding acceptance criteria (e.g., <60% remaining wall requires further evaluation).</p>
                                <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> NFT Calibration Report with Amplitude-Thickness Curve </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">4</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Systematic Tube Scanning</h3>
                        <p>Insert NFT probe into tube inlet (header connection, handhole, or cut tube end) and pneumatically or manually drive through full accessible tube length at controlled speed (0.5-1.0 m/s typical). Acquire continuous amplitude data with encoder position tracking. Technician monitors real-time strip chart display—amplitude drops indicate wall loss; sharp signals indicate dents, welds, or support plates. Mark tube on boiler tube layout map with condition classification: GREEN (>80% wall, acceptable), YELLOW (60-80%, monitor), ORANGE (40-60%, consider replacement), RED (<40%, plug or replace immediately). Repeat for all tubes in scope—typical power plant waterwall/economizer inspection: 500-2000 tubes depending on unit size and accessibility.</p>
                                <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Raw NFT Data Files for All Scanned Tubes </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">5</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Data Analysis & Interpretation</h3>
                        <p>ASNT Level II/III certified analyst reviews all NFT strip charts identifying indications and calculating remaining wall thickness using calibration curve. Classify tube condition per ASME PCC-2 Article 5.1 or plant-specific acceptance criteria. Generate tube-by-tube summary with defect location (distance from header/tubesheet), estimated remaining wall %, and severity classification. Identify patterns indicating systemic degradation mechanisms: uniform thinning = general corrosion/FAC; localized pitting = oxygen corrosion or acid dew point attack; thinning at elbows/bends = erosion; thinning near supports = fretting. Statistical analysis: % tubes degraded by severity category, mean/max wall loss, wastage rate estimation from prior inspection comparison.</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Tube Condition Assessment Report with Defect Map </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">6</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Tube Plugging & Repair Recommendations</h3>
                        <p>Identify tubes requiring immediate action based on remaining wall thickness and fitness-for-service evaluation: PLUG NOW (<40% wall or <2.5mm remaining for typical economizer tubes); REPLACE DURING OUTAGE (40-60% wall, high wastage rate); MONITOR (60-80% wall, re-inspect next outage). Calculate boiler/HRSG performance impact from tube plugging—verify heat transfer capacity, flow distribution, and draft loss remain within acceptable limits (typically <5% plugged tubes acceptable). Provide recommended tube plugging pattern and welding procedures per ASME Section I PW-15. For critical units, recommend IRIS or tube sample removal for precise thickness validation before costly tube replacement decisions. Estimate repair cost and schedule impact: tube plugging (1-2 days, $5K-20K), tube replacement (3-7 days, $50K-200K depending on tube count/access).</p>
                                <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Tube Plugging/Replacement List & Cost Estimate </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">7</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Final Engineering Report & Mitigation Strategy</h3>
                        <p>Comprehensive report includes: executive summary for plant management, equipment/inspection scope description, NFT procedure and calibration data, tube-by-tube condition inventory with color-coded layout maps, statistical analysis and trending (comparison with prior inspections if available), degradation mechanism identification (FAC, fireside erosion, acid corrosion, oxygen pitting, etc.), root cause analysis with contributing factors (chemistry, operating conditions, design issues), fitness-for-service assessment per API 579-1 / ASME FFS-1, repair/replacement recommendations with priorities, mitigation strategies (chemistry optimization, operational changes, metallurgy upgrades, design modifications), and proposed re-inspection interval based on wastage rates. Report signed by ASNT Level III NDT engineer or registered Professional Engineer (PE) for regulatory submissions.</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Final ASME/EPRI-Compliant Inspection Report </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
