    <section class="faq-section">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="section-header center"> <span class="section-label">FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS</span>
                <h2>Near Field Testing Questions Answered</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-container">
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>What is the difference between NFT and conventional eddy current testing (ECT)?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>NFT uses very low frequencies (50-500 Hz) versus conventional ECT (10 kHz - 6 MHz), providing deep penetration through ferromagnetic tube walls where standard ECT struggles. Ferromagnetic materials (carbon steel, low alloy steel) have high magnetic permeability causing strong interference signals in conventional ECT—limiting penetration depth to 1-2mm and obscuring defect signals. NFT's low frequencies minimize permeability effects enabling full wall thickness inspection (3-15mm). Additionally, NFT uses through-transmission detection (separate transmit/receive coils) versus ECT's reflection mode, providing equal sensitivity to ID and OD defects and reduced support plate interference. NFT is specifically optimized for power plant boiler/economizer carbon steel tubes; conventional ECT is better for non-ferromagnetic materials (stainless steel, copper, aluminum).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>Can NFT differentiate between ID and OD defects?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>No, NFT cannot definitively differentiate between internal diameter (ID) and outer diameter (OD) defects because it provides through-wall detection with approximately equal sensitivity to both surfaces. This is both an advantage (balanced defect detection) and limitation (ambiguous defect location). For power plant tubes, ID defects typically originate from waterside/process-side corrosion, and OD defects from fireside/external corrosion—operating history and degradation mechanism knowledge often enable inference of likely defect location. When precise ID vs OD determination is critical for fitness-for-service evaluation or root cause analysis, NFT is supplemented with IRIS ultrasonic testing or tube sample removal/destructive examination providing direct surface identification.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>Does the boiler need to be cool for NFT inspection?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Not necessarily—standard NFT probes operate up to 150°C (300°F), and specialized high-temperature probes enable inspection at tube surface temperatures up to 315°C (600°F). High-temp NFT is valuable for combined cycle HRSG inspections where complete cooldown from operating temperature (450-550°C) to ambient requires 48-72 hours. Performing NFT at reduced load or hot standby (tubes at 200-300°C) saves 2-3 days outage duration worth $2M-5M in lost generation revenue for 400-800 MW combined cycle plant. Thermal imaging camera verification confirms tube surface temperature before probe insertion. For thick-wall tubes (>8mm) or very high-temp service (>315°C), cooldown to <150°C is required for standard probes due to electronic component temperature limits and operator safety considerations.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>How accurate is NFT for measuring remaining tube wall thickness?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>NFT provides ±10% remaining wall thickness accuracy using calibrated signal amplitude analysis—sufficient for screening and tube plugging decisions. Accuracy depends on: (1) Calibration quality (reference tubes with known wall loss), (2) Material consistency (conductivity/permeability variations affect signals), (3) Defect type (general wall loss more accurate than localized pitting), and (4) Analyst experience. For critical tubes requiring precise thickness for fitness-for-service calculations per API 579-1 or ASME FFS-1, NFT is supplemented with IRIS ultrasonic testing providing ±0.01-0.05mm (±0.0004-0.002") accuracy. Typical approach: NFT screens 100% tubes identifying degraded zones (10-30% of tubes), then IRIS spot-checks flagged tubes for precise thickness verification before tube replacement decisions—optimizing inspection cost and data quality.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>What tube defects can NFT detect?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>NFT detects: (1) General wall loss from uniform corrosion or erosion (waterside FAC, fireside oxidation), (2) Localized thinning zones (acid dew point corrosion, oxygen pitting, erosion at flow disruptions), (3) ID and OD pitting (chloride pitting, oxygen corrosion, under-deposit corrosion), (4) Denting from support plate corrosion causing tube diameter reduction, (5) Some circumferential cracks if oriented perpendicular to coil axis and >10mm length. NFT is less sensitive to: tight axial cracks parallel to tube axis, small isolated pits <15% wall depth, hydrogen damage (internal microcracking without significant wall loss), and creep voids (subsurface damage requiring metallography). For crack detection in boiler tubes, NFT is often combined with rotating probe ECT (with magnetic saturation for ferromagnetics) or tube sample removal for metallographic crack assessment.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>How much does NFT boiler tube inspection cost?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>NFT inspection costs vary by unit size, tube count, and accessibility: Small industrial boiler (100-300 tubes): $15,000-35,000; Medium utility boiler/HRSG (500-1500 tubes): $40,000-80,000; Large utility boiler (2000-5000 tubes): $80,000-150,000+. Cost includes mobilization, multi-channel NFT equipment, 3-6 certified technicians, data analysis, and comprehensive reporting. Compare to consequences of undetected tube degradation: single waterwall tube failure causes 24-72 hour forced outage costing $2M-10M in lost generation revenue plus $200K-1M emergency repair costs. Economizer tube failure requiring 1-2 week outage for tube bundle replacement: $5M-20M total impact. NFT ROI typically exceeds 20:1 by preventing one tube failure per 3-5 year inspection cycle, plus optimizing tube replacement timing (condition-based vs. time-based strategies) saving 30-50% on tube capital expenditures.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>How often should boiler tubes be inspected with NFT?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Inspection frequency depends on degradation mechanisms, wastage rates, unit age, and operating mode per ASME Section I, EPRI guidelines, and Risk-Based Inspection (RBI) methodologies: High-risk units (FAC-prone economizers, cyclic HRSG, high-sulfur coal firing, poor chemistry control): Every 1-3 years; Medium-risk units (moderate degradation rates, stable operation, good chemistry): Every 3-5 years or major outages; Low-risk units (new equipment, benign service, excellent chemistry): Every 5-10 years or as-needed based on operating experience. Many utilities perform baseline NFT at commissioning or after major tube replacement, then follow-up inspections to calculate wastage rates (mm/year), then optimize intervals using degradation trending. Continuous online monitoring systems (permanently installed sensors) are emerging for critical high-risk zones enabling real-time condition assessment. EPRI recommends NFT during every major outage (3-5 year cycle) for economizers due to FAC susceptibility—leading cause of boiler tube failures industry-wide.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>Can NFT inspect finned tubes (economizers, HRSG)?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes, NFT can inspect finned tubes but with limitations. Probe must traverse through tube interior (ID inspection) avoiding external fins. Finned tube inspection considerations: (1) Probe diameter selection—must clear internal diameter with minimal gap for good electromagnetic coupling, (2) Fin attachment method—integral fins (extruded, rolled) affect tube wall electromagnetic properties requiring calibration on actual finned tube samples; welded or mechanically attached fins have minimal impact, (3) Fin density—closely-spaced fins (<5mm pitch) may interfere with electromagnetic field requiring specialized probe design, (4) Tube access—fins at tube inlets/outlets may require grinding for probe insertion. Many economizers and HRSG evaporators use finned tubes for compact heat transfer—NFT is routinely performed with fin-optimized probes and procedures. Alternative: External NFT probes designed to scan between fin gaps are available but less common and require specialized calibration.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
